  <section>
    <p class="post-lead"><strong>The one rule:</strong> delivering Telegram Stars or Premium requires your <em>public username</em> and nothing else. Not your password, not your SMS login code, not your 2FA password, not access to your account. Any service asking for those is not selling you anything — it is taking the account. Everything else on this page is detail; that rule catches almost every scam.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Why a username is genuinely enough</h2>
    <p>Telegram has a built-in gift mechanism. A sender specifies a recipient by username, pays, and Telegram delivers the Stars or the subscription to that account. The sender never touches the recipient's account and could not if they wanted to.</p>
    <p>This is the same mechanism a friend uses to gift you Premium. A reseller is doing exactly that, at scale. Which means the moment anyone needs <em>more</em> than a username, the transaction has stopped being a delivery and become something else.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Warning signs, in order of severity</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Asks for your login code.</strong> Stop immediately. This is account theft in progress, always, with no exceptions and no legitimate explanation.</li>
      <li><strong>Asks for your password or 2FA password.</strong> Same category.</li>
      <li><strong>Asks you to log in to a website with your Telegram number.</strong> A phishing page that harvests the code as you enter it.</li>
      <li><strong>A price far below everyone else.</strong> Stars cost the seller real money. A rate at half the market is a bait figure that changes at checkout, or a payment you never see again.</li>
      <li><strong>Pressure and a countdown.</strong> "Pay in 2 minutes or the price goes up." Urgency exists to stop you checking.</li>
      <li><strong>A card number sent in chat rather than shown in your order.</strong> Legitimate flows display the receiving card inside your own order window. A number pasted into a message can be swapped for anyone's.</li>
      <li><strong>No support before payment.</strong> If nobody answers a simple question now, nobody will answer after your money is gone.</li>
      <li><strong>No reviews, or reviews with no dates.</strong> Undated testimonials cost nothing to fabricate.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What a legitimate purchase looks like</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>You open the bot and pick what you want. No registration, no password, no document.</li>
      <li>You enter a username — the recipient's, or your own.</li>
      <li>The order window shows a card number and an exact amount. Both come from your own order, not from a chat message.</li>
      <li>You transfer that exact amount from any app you use — Click, Payme, Uzum or your bank.</li>
      <li>You confirm, and delivery arrives in about 10 seconds through Telegram's official notification.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Note what is absent: at no point does anyone need to be inside your account.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How to check a service before paying</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Look for dated reviews.</strong> Names and dates on individual reviews are far harder to fake than a round number in a headline. StarsJoy publishes its reviews with dates on <a href="/sharhlar">the reviews page</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Treat unverifiable scale claims as worthless.</strong> "100,000+ orders" with nothing to check it against tells you nothing — and inconsistent figures (a huge order count next to a small user count) tell you something worse.</li>
      <li><strong>Ask a question first.</strong> A real service answers before payment. Response quality now predicts response quality later.</li>
      <li><strong>Check there is a public offer or terms page</strong> naming what happens if delivery fails.</li>
      <li><strong>Send a small first order.</strong> 50 Stars costs 12,000 UZS. That is a cheap way to test a service you have not used before.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>If you already gave something away</h2>
    <p>Act in minutes, not hours:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Settings → Privacy and Security → Two-Step Verification</strong> — set or change the cloud password. This locks out anyone holding only a login code.</li>
      <li><strong>Settings → Devices</strong> — terminate all other sessions.</li>
      <li>Check <strong>Settings → Privacy and Security → Active Sessions</strong> again afterwards; if a session reappears, someone still has access.</li>
      <li>Report the payment to your bank with the receipt. Card-to-card transfers are hard to reverse, but a report creates a record.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>If you lost money but not the account, you got off lightly. The account is the more expensive thing.</p>
  </section>

